      <title>Linux Containers and OpenVZ</title>
      <subtitle></subtitle>
      <track>Hypervisors Track</track>
      <type></type>
      <language></language>
      <abstract>&#xA;&lt;p&gt;The talk is about operating system virtualization technology known as OpenVZ. This is an effective way of partitioning a Linux machine into multiple isolated Linux containers. All containers are running on top of one single Linux kernel, which results in excellent density, performance and manageability.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;The talk gives an overall description of OpenVZ building blocks, such as namespaces, cgroups and various resource controllers. A few features, notably live migration and virtual swap, are described in greater details. Results of some performance measurements against VMware, Xen and KVM are given. Finally, we will provide a status update on merging bits and pieces of OpenVZ kernel to upstream Linux kernel, and share our plans for the future.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;The talk is suitable for general audience, but will be of most interest to people interested in Linux kernel, resource management, virtualization, and cloud computing.&lt;/p&gt;      </abstract>
